public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
    List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
    if (nums.length == 1) {
        List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
        list.add(nums[0]);
        result.add(list);
        return result;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        int[] remainingNums = new int[nums.length - 1];
        System.arraycopy(nums, 0, remainingNums, 0, i);
        System.arraycopy(nums, i + 1, remainingNums, i, nums.length - i - 1);
        for (List<Integer> perm : permute(remainingNums)) {
            List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
            list.add(nums[i]);
            list.addAll(perm);
            result.add(list);
        }
    }
    return result;
}
```

This is a recursive solution to the problem of finding all possible permutations of an array of distinct integers. The method takes an array of integers as input and returns a list of lists of integers representing all possible permutations of the input array.

The method first checks if the input array has only one element, in which case the method returns a list containing only that element.

If the input array has more than one element, the method uses the `Arrays.copyOfRange` method to split the array into two subarrays: one containing the first element and the remaining elements, and another containing the second element and the remaining elements. It then recursively calls itself for each subarray, and combines the results into a single list of lists of integers.

For example, if the input array is `[1, 2, 3]`, the method will first split it into two subarrays: `[1, 2]` and `[3]`. It will then call itself for each subarray, and the result will be a list of lists of integers representing all possible permutations of the subarrays:
